29
00:02:18.921 --> 00:02:24.053
Power shapes consultation even when
everyone intends to help. Think privately

30
00:02:24.054 --> 00:02:29.254
about a meeting you've been part of. Who
chose the time, place and agenda? Who

31
00:02:29.255 --> 00:02:34.455
understood the language being used? Who
could make a decision? The slide names

32
00:02:34.456 --> 00:02:39.318
several influences: socio-economic
circumstances, historical and cultural

33
00:02:39.319 --> 00:02:44.047
experiences of education, language,
understanding of school systems and

34
00:02:44.048 --> 00:02:49.315
curriculum, and experiences of school
itself. In many meetings, the school sets

35
00:02:49.316 --> 00:02:54.584
the arrangements and several staff are
present. A family may arrive with a long

36
00:02:54.585 --> 00:02:59.109
history of meetings, including experiences
in which they didn't feel

37
00:02:59.110 --> 00:03:03.972
heard. Online and phone meetings can also
create barriers through timing,

38
00:03:03.973 --> 00:03:08.903
technology or how people take turns.
Partnership can be difficult for more

39
00:03:08.904 --> 00:03:14.104
than one person. We can acknowledge that
and still examine what the school can

40
00:03:14.105 --> 00:03:19.507
change. Whose knowledge is heard? Is the
student included in a way that works for

41
00:03:19.508 --> 00:03:24.776
them? Those questions help us examine the
process without labelling a family as

42
00:03:24.777 --> 00:03:26.397
difficult or disengaged.

43
00:03:27.921 --> 00:03:33.174
On page twenty, use the Power dynamics
activity. Think of an imbalance from a

44
00:03:33.175 --> 00:03:38.289
home perspective, an imbalance from a
school perspective, and something you

45
00:03:38.290 --> 00:03:43.612
could change to address them. You can
write privately. The slide also offers a

46
00:03:43.613 --> 00:03:48.520
shared whiteboard or chat for a live
online group. If your group has one

47
00:03:48.521 --> 00:03:53.635
available and you want to contribute, use
general examples that identify no

48
00:03:53.636 --> 00:03:58.889
student, family or staff member. This
recording doesn't require a whiteboard.

49
00:03:58.890 --> 00:04:04.557
Consider school language, meeting timing,
who sets the agenda and how decisions are

50
00:04:04.558 --> 00:04:09.810
explained. Look for a process you can
improve, rather than a person to blame.

51
00:04:09.811 --> 00:04:15.341
Choose one change you could carry into a
later conversation plan. Pause the video

52
00:04:15.342 --> 00:04:18.863
for a few minutes to work through the
three columns.

53
00:04:26.881 --> 00:04:31.225
Look at the solution you've noted. Would
it make it easier for someone to

54
00:04:31.226 --> 00:04:36.052
contribute their knowledge or influence a
decision? Sending clear questions ahead

55
00:04:36.053 --> 00:04:40.517
of time, explaining unfamiliar terms or
agreeing the agenda together may be

56
00:04:40.518 --> 00:04:44.983
