63
00:07:03.081 --> 00:07:08.816
Take a moment with the questions on page
twenty. What decisions were made, and who

64
00:07:08.817 --> 00:07:13.772
made them? What impact did those decisions
have on Adon? Then return to

65
00:07:13.773 --> 00:07:19.366
the students you have in mind. How are
their preferences asked and heard, and by

66
00:07:19.367 --> 00:07:24.606
which of their teachers? You can keep this
reflection private, or share one

67
00:07:24.607 --> 00:07:30.200
general point in your live group's chat if
you choose. Pause here for more time.

68
00:07:38.201 --> 00:07:44.059
The practical question is how a student's
view can influence what happens. Ask in a

69
00:07:44.060 --> 00:07:48.990
way they can use, and keep their own words
or communication accurately

70
00:07:48.991 --> 00:07:53.849
represented. What helps? What is hard?
What should teachers know? For

71
00:07:53.850 --> 00:07:59.422
adolescents, being asked can lead into
supported participation in meetings and,

72
00:07:59.423 --> 00:08:04.995
over time, taking more of a lead where
they choose. We'll look at that shortly.

73
00:08:06.521 --> 00:08:11.067
Useful consultation has a clear shared
purpose. It uses open questions, more

74
00:08:11.068 --> 00:08:15.553
than one way to contribute and respect for
student and family knowledge. We

75
00:08:15.554 --> 00:08:20.220
record what we've heard accurately and
agree an action, who is responsible and

76
00:08:20.221 --> 00:08:25.070
how we'll follow up. The checklist is on
page twenty-one. Consider the difference

77
00:08:25.071 --> 00:08:29.920
between asking, Any concerns? and, What
does a good morning look like? The second

78
00:08:29.921 --> 00:08:33.860
question invites detail that may otherwise
remain unseen. A useful

79
00:08:33.861 --> 00:08:38.407
follow-up tells the student or family what
you did with what they shared and

80
00:08:38.408 --> 00:08:42.711
checks whether you've understood it
correctly. The slide also reminds us

81
00:08:42.712 --> 00:08:47.561
that, under the Disability Standards for
Education 2005, schools must consult the

82
00:08:47.562 --> 00:08:52.471
student, or an associate such as a parent
or carer, about adjustments. Follow your

83
00:08:52.472 --> 00:08:57.078
school and system processes alongside
current official guidance. Ask, listen,

84
00:08:57.079 --> 00:09:01.867
and follow up. A form or tool can support
that conversation, but it can't do the

85
00:09:01.868 --> 00:09:02.898
listening for us.

86
00:09:04.401 --> 00:09:08.853
The two columns offer practical ways to
make consultation accessible. With

87
00:09:08.854 --> 00:09:13.428
students, prepare the conversation and
make communication choices available.

88
00:09:13.429 --> 00:09:18.187
Questions, agendas or simple choices can
be shared beforehand. Allow processing

89
00:09:18.188 --> 00:09:22.762
time and consider a sensory environment
that works for them. A student might

90
00:09:22.763 --> 00:09:27.093
contribute through speech, A A C, writing,
drawing, a video or a trusted

91
00:09:27.094 --> 00:09:31.546
supporter. That person's role is to assist
the student's communication and

92
00:09:31.547 --> 00:09:36.304
decision-making. Keep the student's own
view at the centre. Notice behaviour as

93
00:09:36.305 --> 00:09:41.124
possible communication, while checking
meaning rather than assuming you know it.

94
00:09:41.125 --> 00:09:45.760
With families, begin with strengths,
interests and what works well. Use plain

95
00:09:45.761 --> 00:09:50.579
language, explain educational terms and
listen without assuming that your school

96
00:09:50.580 --> 00:09:55.276
perspective is complete. Families may have
different cultural experiences, and

97
00:09:55.277 --> 00:09:59.791
some parents may be neurodivergent
themselves. Use specific examples, check

98
00:09:59.792 --> 00:10:04.244
shared understanding and agree what
happens next. Choose one practice from

99
00:10:04.245 --> 00:10:08.208
each column that could improve the
conversation you are preparing.

100
00:10:09.721 --> 00:10:14.572
Adolescents can move from being talked
about, to taking part and, over time,

101
00:10:14.573 --> 00:10:19.683
leading their own meetings. That requires
preparation and support in a form that

102
00:10:19.684 --> 00:10:24.276
works for them. A student might attend
part of a meeting, send a written

103
00:10:24.277 --> 00:10:29.258
contribution, share a one-page profile,
receive questions in advance, or ask a

104
00:10:29.259 --> 00:10:34.175
trusted adult to support them. Joining an
online meeting with a camera off or

105
00:10:34.176 --> 00:10:39.092
contributing through chat can also be
valid choices. Being physically present

106
00:10:39.093 --> 00:10:43.685
without preparation doesn't guarantee
participation. Choosing writing or

107
00:10:43.686 --> 00:10:48.602
deciding not to attend a particular
meeting doesn't mean a student has failed

108
00:10:48.603 --> 00:10:53.389
at self-advocacy. We need to understand
the choice and make workable routes

109
00:10:53.390 --> 00:10:58.435
available. Speaking up for themselves can
be useful practice for senior school,

110
00:10:58.436 --> 00:11:03.611
work and life after school. A student who
spends the day masking may first need a

111
00:11:03.612 --> 00:11:08.787
safer setting and time to prepare. Where
you can, ask the student directly, using

112
00:11:08.788 --> 00:11:13.445
their preferred ways of communicating.
Their learning and support are the

113
00:11:13.446 --> 00:11:15.060
subject of the decisions.

114
00:11:16.561 --> 00:11:22.082
This slide touches on distress, and you
are welcome to pause or step away. When

115
00:11:22.083 --> 00:11:27.532
capacity runs out, the response may be
outward or inward. A meltdown can be an

116
00:11:27.533 --> 00:11:33.266
outward response to overwhelm. A shutdown
can be inward, quiet and easier to miss.

117
00:11:33.267 --> 00:11:38.292
School Can't describes difficulty
attending that needs understanding and

118
00:11:38.293 --> 00:11:43.672
support, rather than being treated as
wilful defiance. In secondary school, a

119
00:11:43.673 --> 00:11:48.557
shutdown might be mistaken for
disengagement. Difficulty attending may

120
00:11:48.558 --> 00:11:53.937
develop over time, with partial days or
growing dread of school. We shouldn't

121
00:11:53.938 --> 00:11:59.600
infer any one explanation from a single
sign. The question is what the student is

122
00:11:59.601 --> 00:12:05.192
experiencing and what support is needed.
Home and school may see different parts

123
00:12:05.193 --> 00:12:10.572
of that picture. School sees demands
across periods. Families may see what is

124
00:12:10.573 --> 00:12:15.881
left at the end of the day. Focus on
safety, what came before, what supports

125
00:12:15.882 --> 00:12:21.049
recovery and what can change in the
environment, task or response. Use the

126
00:12:21.050 --> 00:12:26.358
appropriate school support processes and
relevant team. We don't need crisis

127
00:12:26.359 --> 00:12:29.330
stories in a group chat to learn from
this.

128
00:12:30.841 --> 00:12:35.642
Let's prepare for one purposeful
conversation. On page twenty-one, use the

129
00:12:35.643 --> 00:12:40.772
Bridge to the next family conversation
section. Return to a student you have in

130
00:12:40.773 --> 00:12:45.705
mind and your Session Three design change.
What needs to be understood? What

131
00:12:45.706 --> 00:12:51.098
question could open the conversation? How
will the student take part, or have their

132
00:12:51.099 --> 00:12:56.031
views included? What could be decided
together? How and when will the school

133
00:12:56.032 --> 00:13:01.096
follow up? Bring in the process change
from your power reflection. Perhaps the

134
00:13:01.097 --> 00:13:06.226
person needs information in advance or a
different way to contribute. Keep your

135
00:13:06.227 --> 00:13:11.357
notes about preparation open and curious.
You aren't scripting what a family or

136
00:13:11.358 --> 00:13:16.750
student should say. Pause the video for a
few minutes to write, then keep this page

137
00:13:16.751 --> 00:13:19.182
open for the later conversation plan.

138
00:13:27.201 --> 00:13:31.822
As you come back, check the question
you've chosen. Does it invite knowledge

139
00:13:31.823 --> 00:13:36.505
you don't yet have, or does it steer
someone towards a decision already made?

140
00:13:36.506 --> 00:13:41.435
A shared purpose gives the conversation
direction while leaving room for the plan

141
00:13:41.436 --> 00:13:45.871
to change. Keep one clear question and one
way the student's view will be

142
00:13:45.872 --> 00:13:46.426
included.

143
00:13:47.961 --> 00:13:53.001
Belonging, capacity and joy help us judge
whether a change supports flourishing.

144
00:13:53.002 --> 00:13:57.339
Belonging means being recognised, included
and able to participate as

145
00:13:57.340 --> 00:14:01.933
oneself. Capacity concerns what the day
costs, protects and restores. Joy

146
00:14:01.934 --> 00:14:06.655
includes interests, connection and
authentic expression. Keep them together

147
00:14:06.656 --> 00:14:11.249
as you review the design. Reducing a
barrier matters. So does whether the

148
00:14:11.250 --> 00:14:16.226
student feels more included, has more
available for the rest of the day, or can

149
00:14:16.227 --> 00:14:20.947
take part in something they value. Grades,
attendance and a quiet classroom

150
00:14:20.948 --> 00:14:26.052
response won't tell us everything. Across
different periods and teachers, we need

151
00:14:26.053 --> 00:14:31.092
to keep asking about the student's whole
experience. These questions connect our

152
00:14:31.093 --> 00:14:33.517
lens, learning design and partnership.

153
00:14:35.041 --> 00:14:40.076
Turn to page twenty-two, Start, Continue,
Reconsider. This plan is yours. Choose

154
00:14:40.077 --> 00:14:44.793
one small strategy to start next week, one
practice to continue because the

155
00:14:44.794 --> 00:14:49.510
series confirmed its value, and one
approach you would now reconsider. Your

156
00:14:49.511 --> 00:14:54.355
Session Three this week change can be your
Start. Make it specific and within

157
00:14:54.356 --> 00:14:59.136
your influence. Ask what barrier or
unnecessary demand it addresses. Include

158
00:14:59.137 --> 00:15:04.108
who you need to consult and when you will
review it. For each part of the plan,

159
00:15:04.109 --> 00:15:08.953
decide how you will know whether it helps.
What could you learn about access,

160
00:15:08.954 --> 00:15:13.351
participation or the student's experience?
Keep the student and family

161
00:15:13.352 --> 00:15:17.877
perspective in that evidence. The aim
isn't for a student to appear less

162
00:15:17.878 --> 00:15:22.530
Autistic. Reconsidering a practice is part
of learning. You can look again

163
00:15:22.531 --> 00:15:27.694
without blaming yourself or anyone else.
Pause here for about seven minutes, or as

164
00:15:27.695 --> 00:15:32.283
long as you need. Sharing one general
point with a live group is entirely

165
00:15:32.284 --> 00:15:32.857
optional.

166
00:15:40.881 --> 00:15:46.094
Review your Start as one sentence. Is it
small enough to begin and clear enough to

167
00:15:46.095 --> 00:15:50.857
review? Make sure you know who else should
be involved before acting. If it

168
00:15:50.858 --> 00:15:55.943
