to plan. Keep all notes anonymous. You

183
00:17:02.692 --> 00:17:07.375
can check an opening sentence with a
colleague if you wish, but keep the

184
00:17:07.376 --> 00:17:10.475
response unscripted. Leave real space to
listen.

185
00:17:18.480 --> 00:17:22.963
Read your opening sentence back to
yourself. Does it make the purpose clear

186
00:17:22.964 --> 00:17:27.811
and invite the other person's knowledge?
Check what you are offering against your

187
00:17:27.812 --> 00:17:32.477
own role and what you can realistically
follow through on. If it's a handover,

188
00:17:32.478 --> 00:17:37.143
ask what useful guidance and student voice
need to travel to the next teacher.

189
00:17:37.144 --> 00:17:41.627
If it's with a family or student, make
sure the conversation can change the

190
00:17:41.628 --> 00:17:46.293
plan. Agreement with an already finished
school decision shouldn't be the only

191
00:17:46.294 --> 00:17:49.686
possible outcome. Keep the next step and
follow-up clear.

192
00:17:51.200 --> 00:17:56.293
For the final reflection, turn to page
twenty-four. Then look back at page four,

193
00:17:56.294 --> 00:18:00.935
where you wrote what you hoped to
understand at the start of Session One.

194
00:18:00.936 --> 00:18:05.965
If you don't have those notes, simply
think back to your starting point. What's

195
00:18:05.966 --> 00:18:10.929
different now? Where were you at the
beginning, and where are you now? You may

196
00:18:10.930 --> 00:18:15.507
have more questions as well as more
understanding. Seeing complexity you

197
00:18:15.508 --> 00:18:20.343
hadn't noticed before can be part of
learning. Keep this reflection private.

198
00:18:20.344 --> 00:18:24.857
Finish with one sentence for yourself:
what will I do differently in my

199
00:18:24.858 --> 00:18:29.048
teaching? Pause the video to give that
